\lang1033 \emdash  To represent it, English knows only of the infinitive (}{\i\f211\lang1033 to swim}{\f208\lang1033 ) and the gerund (}{\i\f211\lang1033 swimming}{\f208\lang1033 ). Latin, on the contrary, is much richer. We will take as an example the verb }{\b\f210\lang1033 pisc}{\f208\lang1033 a}{\b\f210\lang1033 ri}{\f208\lang1033  (}{\i\f211\lang1033 to fish}{\f208\lang1033 ), (}{\b\f210\lang1033 p}{\f208\lang1033 i}{\b\f210\lang1033 scor}{\f208\lang1033 , }{\b\f210\lang1033 pisc}{\f208\lang1033 a}{\b\f210\lang1033 ris}{\f208\lang1033 , etc., deponent vb., 1}{\f208\lang1033\super st}{\f208\lang1033  conjugation), a verb which we will otherwise find in a forthcoming lesson.\par \par 1}{\f208\lang1033\super st}{\f208\lang1033  }{\scaps\f208\lang1033 If the action is the subject of the sentence}{\f208\lang1033  (nominative), we use the }{\scaps\f208\lang1033 infinitive}{\f208\lang1033  as in English (although English can also use the gerund in this sense): }{\b\f210\lang1033 D}{\f208\lang1033 u}{\b\f210\lang1033 lce est pisc}{\f208\lang1033 a}{\b\f210\lang1033 ri}{\f208\lang1033 : }{\i\f211\lang1033 it is pleasant to fish}{\f208\lang1033  (}{\i\f211\lang1033 to fish is pleasant, fishing is pleasant}{\f208\lang1033 ).\par \par 2}{\f208\lang1033\super nd}{\f208\lang1033  The same goes if it takes the place of a }{\scaps\f208\lang1033 direct object}{\f208\lang1033  (accusative): }{\b\f210\lang1033 Pisc}{\f208\lang1033 a}{\b\f210\lang1033 ri n}{\f208\lang1033 o}{\b\f210\lang1033 lo}{\f208\lang1033 : }{\i\f211\lang1033 I do not want to fish}{\f208\lang1033 .\par \par N.B.\endash  The }{\i\f211\lang1033 present}{\f208\lang1033  infinitive does not decline. For the moment, let us leave aside the future and past infinitives.\par \par 3}{\f208\lang1033\super rd}{\f208\lang1033  If the action is the }{\scaps\f208\lang1033 complement of a noun}{\f208\lang1033  or }{\scaps\f208\lang1033 of an adjective}{\f208\lang1033  or it is an }{\scaps\f208\lang1033 adverbia